Looking at this problem, I need to determine whether two projections in the Cuntz algebra $\mathcal{O}_n$ with the same $K_0$ class are necessarily Murray–von Neumann equivalent.

**Answer: No.** Here is the proof.

---

## Proof

Let $\mathcal{O}_n$ be the Cuntz algebra generated by isometries $s_1, \dots, s_n$ satisfying $\sum_{i=1}^n s_i s_i^* = 1$. Recall the classical result of Cuntz:

$$K_0(\mathcal{O}_n) \cong \mathbb{Z}/(n-1)\mathbb{Z}, \quad \text{generated by } [1].$$

### The counterexample

Set $e = 0$ (the zero projection) and $f = 1 - s_n s_n^*$.

**Same $K_0$ class.** Since $s_n$ is an isometry, $s_n^* s_n = 1$ and $s_n s_n^*$ is a projection with $s_n s_n^* \sim 1$ (Murray–von Neumann), witnessed by the partial isometry $s_n$ itself. Therefore in $K_0$:

$$[f] = [1 - s_n s_n^*] = [1] - [s_n s_n^*] = [1] - [1] = 0 = [e].$$

So $e$ and $f$ have the same $K_0$ class.

**Not Murray–von Neumann equivalent.** The projection $f = 1 - s_n s_n^* = \sum_{i=1}^{n-1} s_i s_i^*$ is a sum of $n-1$ nonzero, mutually orthogonal range projections, hence $f \neq 0$. On the other hand $e = 0$. Murray–von Neumann equivalence preserves the property of being zero: if $v^* v = 0$ then $v = 0$ and so $v v^* = 0$. Therefore $e \not\sim f$.

### Conclusion

The projections $e = 0$ and $f = 1 - s_n s_n^*$ in $\mathcal{O}_n$ have the same $K_0$ class but are not Murray–von Neumann equivalent. $\blacksquare$

---

**Remark.** The obstruction is purely the zero projection. Cuntz proved that the Murray–von Neumann equivalence classes of **nonzero** projections in $\mathcal{O}_n$ are in bijection with $K_0(\mathcal{O}_n) \cong \mathbb{Z}/(n-1)\mathbb{Z}$. The issue is that $K_0(\mathcal{O}_n)$ has torsion (for every $n \geq 2$), so the zero element of $K_0$ is also represented by nonzero projections—namely any $P_F = \sum_{\alpha \in F} s_\alpha s_\alpha^*$ with $|F| \equiv 0 \pmod{n-1}$ and $|F| \geq 1$—and these are not equivalent to $0$.

### PROOF COMPLETE
